## Intuition
Maintaining a Deque of size k which stores indices (this intuition is from he problem to return maximum in the sliding window)  ,and a hashmap to store frequency of elements in that window.

## Approach
 NoW as we parse we will check if the element is already present in the map then we will add its index in the deque from last and increase its frequency in the map, else if the element is not present,we will add it in the map with  frequency of 1 and also add it in the deque from last;
 after every iteration we will check if the current index>=(k-1) [meaning has it reached the window size] if yes then we add map.size() to the ans and continue;

 for maintaining the window of size k ,on every index we check if dq.peekFist() [which will hold the leftmost index of that window] <=(i-k) ;if yes we poll from first of deque and decrease the element's frequency from the map [if the freq of element becomes 0 ,then we remove it]

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> distinctNumbers(int[] nums, int k) {
        List<Integer> ans=new ArrayList<>(Collections.nCopies(nums.length-k+1,0));
        Deque<Integer> dq=new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Integer,Integer> mp=new  HashMap<>();
        int index=0;
        int n=nums.length;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
           //maintain size of the dq
           while(!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst()<=(i-k)){
            int num=nums[dq.peekFirst()];
            dq.pollFirst();
            mp.put(num,mp.get(num)-1);
            if(mp.get(num)==0)mp.remove(num);
           }

           //add in dq and increase freq in Map
           int num=nums[i];
           if(mp.containsKey(num)){
            dq.offerLast(i);
            mp.put(num,mp.get(num)+1);
           }else{
            mp.put(num,1);
            dq.offerLast(i);
           }
           if(i>=(k-1)){
            ans.set(index,mp.size());
           index++;
           }

        }
        return ans;
    }
}

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n) where n is the length of the input array. Each index is added to and removed from the deque exactly once, and map operations (put, get, remove) run in amortized O(1). The initialization of the ArrayList is O(n-k+1), which is O(n).

**Space Complexity:**
O(n) where n is the number of elements in the array. The HashMap stores at most min(n, k) distinct elements, the deque stores at most k elements, and the results list stores n-k+1 elements
